WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.280
Villains. Often times, the best part of a
movie. Can’t live with them, can’t feed

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:15.670
‘em to a pack of rabid hyenas. Usually.
Villains are the people we love to hate or

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:18.430
fear, with the occasional modicum of empathy.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:22.100
But something has been happening with villains
lately. If you’ve found yourself nodding

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.950
along to the logic of a diabolical plot, or
feeling more empathetic than usual towards

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.220
a film’s antagonist, you’re not alone.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.360
Today’s villains, from Killmonger in Black
Panther to Screenslaver in The Incredibles

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.170
2 have become weirdly relatable, each equipped
with their own cogent critiques of the world.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.290
"People will trade quality for ease every
time."

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:48.680
Villainy is and has always been a reflection
of value systems and fears of the time. So

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.739
how did we get from this to this, and what
does it say about our past, and our world

00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:59.010
today? We’ll find out in this Wisecrack
Edition on the Cinematic History of Villainy.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:33.650
And 
a warning, there’s a ton of spoilers here,

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:42.120
for movies old and new.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:47.070
To understand how we got here, we have to
explore multiple eras and see how film villains

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:52.060
embody the spirit and anxieties of the times.
Now, a quick disclaimer, none of these trends

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:56.479
are absolute. Villains can break the mold,
or jump back into the past for inspiration,

00:01:56.479 --> 00:02:01.180
but what we’re discussing here are noticeable
trends among some of the most enduring films

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.460
of each era. We’re going to start our journey
from the simple mustache-twirling villain

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.720
to the complex, philosophizing radical by
looking at the 1950s and the early 1960s,

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.880
the height of the war that wasn’t hot.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.879
In the post-World War II, party-in-the-suburbs
era of American history, describing our world

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:23.349
seemed pretty simple. We were the heroes who
saved the world from the evil Nazis and were

00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:29.400
now tasked with staving off the also evil
U.S.S.R. It was very black and white, and

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.569
so were our villains. Well yeah, literally,
but also metaphorically. Movie screens were

00:02:34.569 --> 00:02:39.849
full of bad Russian people, bad Asian people,
and very bad aliens.

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:44.470
What do all of these naughty dudes have in
common? They’re the “other” - an inherent,

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:49.519
inexplicable evil that’s stoked on destroying
America and our apple pie values. Sometimes

00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:53.800
that “Other” took the form of an amorphous
red-for-commie Blob ]or the Creature from

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:58.310
the Black Lagoon. Other times, the “other”
was an alien hatched in a pod and designed

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:01.750
to look exactly like you, as in The Invasion
of the Body Snatchers — "Where do they come

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:06.239
from?" — which some critics say represented
the fear of Communist brainwashing and infiltration.

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:20.020
Also in the 50s, the movie industry went way
back to basics, producing a number of biblical

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:26.129
sagas with running times of four hours or
more. These expensive epics proved massively

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:30.709
popular, with three ranking in the top ten
highest-grossing films of the decade. Perhaps

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:35.349
this was partially because they so perfectly
embodied Cold War propaganda, which depicted

00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:40.400
a face-off between God-fearing Americans and
the godless communists. Ben Hur showed an

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.220
aristocratic Jew being persecuted by an evil
Roman commander, while Quo Vadis pits new

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:55.220
Christian converts against the evil Emperor
Nero and his Roman empire, and The Ten Commandments

00:03:55.220 --> 00:04:00.400
depicts good ol’ Moses staring down the
Egyptian emperor Ramses. These films all portrayed

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.519
the noble “Judeo-Christian” taking on
a cruel, powerful despot of a non-monotheistic

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:09.670
religion. These villains were representatives
of larger groups of bad people who wanted

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:20.060
to cancel baseball and tupperware parties.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:25.010
Even when villains weren’t explicitly foreign
or pagan, they were just anti-American, Communist

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:29.810
double agents as seen in a flurry of Cold
War propaganda films. Later, spy mania of

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:34.979
the 1960s would also reflect Cold War anxiety,
with James Bond facing off against various

00:04:34.979 --> 00:04:42.990
agents of SMERSH, the imaginary Russian intelligence
agency.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:48.050
What all of these movies had in common was
an us vs. them mentality, with no-good Commies

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:55.460
portrayed as pure and simply evil. One-dimensional
foreign/communist/alien/blob villains took

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:00.300
a back seat in the late 60s and 70s
as we saw a definitive shift in the portrayal

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:04.990
of villains. It was fitting for a time of
major social turbulence and soul-searching,

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:09.460
as the Civil Rights Movement and Vietnam made
visible some of the not-so-perfect aspects

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.080
of America like racism and war.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.490
This period also saw a startling drop in public
trust of government a phenomenon that changed

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:22.900
movie villains dramatically as outrage over
the war met outrage about Nixon’s tapes.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:27.930
Notably, 1970s movies shined a light on the
corrupt social systems that Americans were

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:33.521
increasingly disillusioned with. Evil politicians
and public officials loomed large. Popular

00:05:33.521 --> 00:05:37.759
political thriller All the President’s Men
brought the Watergate scandal to the big screen

00:05:37.759 --> 00:05:39.199
in 1976.

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:44.570
Fictional accounts also touched on institutional
corruption, like in Steven Spielberg’s Jaws,

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:48.860
which depicts a crooked mayor who cares more
about tourism revenue than keeping people

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:59.760
from becoming shark bait. Further depictions
of the evil and mighty came in Chinatown,

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.110
in which the sinister billionaire rejects
audience’s judgements of him by claiming

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:08.460
that his evil acts were brought about by his
environment. “Most people never face the

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:14.330
fact that in the right place, at the right
time, they’re capable of anything.”

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:20.290
The suggestion is that circumstances of power
make the villain, rather than any innate nature.

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:24.680
Corrupt cop films were another popular way
of portraying the power struggles taking place.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:29.539
Serpico, based on a true story, depicts a
world in which police corruption is an assumed

00:06:29.539 --> 00:06:35.350
fact. "You fire without looking? You fire
without a warning, without a fucking brain

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:39.870
in your head?" In this world, bribes and brutality
are the coffee and donuts of police work,

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:47.889
so much so that Al Pacino’s Serpico is distrusted
because he’s not willing to accept bribes.

00:06:47.889 --> 00:06:52.460
Corrupt cops were mainstays on the big screen,
popping up in The Godfather, White Lightning,The

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:59.090
French Connection and The Conversation. Institutions,
it seems, had lost their presumption of goodwill.

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:03.289
As a result, we started seeing villains who
embody the very broken systems people were

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:07.940
angry about. Take Nurse Ratched from One Flew
Over the Cuckoo’s Nest, who turned a benevolent

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:18.290
profession into one that turns vivid individuals
into drugged-up, dependent ghosts of themselves.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:23.800
In a similarly bleak depiction of institutionalization,
East from Alcatraz renders prisoners sympathetic

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:28.460
heroes struggling against the efforts of an
evil warden. Something similar can be seen

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:29.800
in Cool Hand Luke.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:36.910
In both cases, the person tasked with imposing
law, order or societal norms is recast as

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:42.230
the villain. The 1970s further muddied the
waters by blurring the line between hero and

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:45.890
villain until the distinctions became all
but meaningless. These characters are are

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:51.022
depicted as conflicted, heavily-flawed protagonists
with questionable, even hideous morals. Even

00:07:51.022 --> 00:07:52.022
when they weren’t particularly powerful,
bad guys in the 70s were depicted as conflicted

00:07:52.022 --> 00:07:53.022
individuals struggling to navigate the lines
between good and bad, or alternately villains

00:07:53.022 --> 00:07:54.022
of circumstance.

00:07:54.022 --> 00:07:55.022
You could argue that Walter White of Breaking
Bad starts as an anti-hero - a cancer-stricken

00:07:55.022 --> 00:07:56.022
chemistry teacher who starts dealing meth
to help provide for his family - but eventually

00:07:56.022 --> 00:07:57.022
after say, the 5th ruthless murder, morphs
into a villain protagonist.

00:07:57.022 --> 00:07:58.022
In Martin Scorsese’s 1976 film, Taxi Driver,
Travis Bickle is one such heroic villain,

00:07:58.022 --> 00:08:03.199
or villainous hero, depending on your perspective
swinging on the pendulum of violently bad

00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:08.340
to violently good-ish as he goes from plotting
to gun down a presidential candidate to plotting

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:10.490
to rescue a 12-year-old prostitute.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:15.569
In the previous year’s Dog Day Afternoon,
novice crook and hero/villain Sonny Wortzik

00:08:15.569 --> 00:08:20.440
botches a bank robbery and spontaneously takes
all the workers hostage. However, we soon

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:25.060
learn the reason for Sonny’s dalliance with
crime: He needed the money to help his wife

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:29.010
Leon, get sexual reassignment surgery. "Right
away, Sonny wanted to get me the money for

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:35.180
the operation." We find out that Sonny is
a quality dude who pays his parents rent and

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:39.930
doesn’t typically go around holding up banks.
Not your villain of the past.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:45.410
Meanwhile, one particularly popular series,
Star Wars, stands out as markedly simplistic

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:49.940
in this era of more complex villains. It might
seem to counter our theory, as it presents

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:54.430
a pretty basic, black and white paradigm of
good vs. evil, even if goes against the grain

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:59.290
later on with Ewoks reminiscent of the Viet
Cong.

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:04.680
The 1980s backpedaled hard on the anti villain
by revelling in more cartoonish villains who

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:07.850
directly embodied the biggest threats of the
time.

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:12.720
Effectively, it ushered back in an “us vs.
them” paradigm by tapping into fears surrounding

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:18.050
the War on Drugs. Unsurprisingly, villains
of the time wanted a piece of that sweet sweet

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:23.980
drug money in films like Scarface, To Live
&amp; Die in L.A., Beverly Hills Cop and Raw Deal.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:29.080
This depiction of evil drug lords could be
seen as a ying-yang response to the less-than-savory

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:34.660
cops of the 70s films. Take Tango &amp; Cash,
which depicted two roguish cops who are treated

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:40.700
as irrepressible protagonists, despite engaging
in some of the exact same violence Serpico

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:45.140
condemned. However, they’re portrayed as
the best cops in the city because their unconventional

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:51.260
tactics have cost local drug lord and rat-kissing
villain Yves Perret oodles of money. Then,

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:56.160
said kingpin, as if schooled in the bad-cop
films of the 70s, gets them falsely incriminated

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.570
for murder. The film ultimately comes down
hard on the side of cops and a “by any means

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:06.140
necessary” model of policing. “I’ve
been a policeman for 12 years and I think

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:08.360
it’s the best organization in the country."

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:11.240
At the same time, the decade saw the resurgence
of Cold War anxieties, with some historians

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:13.709
even referring to the eras the Cold War II.
As President Ronald Reagan increased military

00:10:13.709 --> 00:10:18.630
spending and ended arms control, Americans
rediscovered their fear of nuclear war. At

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:22.970
the same time,“evil Russians” had their
moment in the sun with films like Rocky IV,

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:27.570
which remained the highest-grossing sports
flick until 2009. It’s also been accused

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:32.980
of being pure anti-Soviet propaganda because
of its villain, Soviet boxer Ivan Drago, who

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:36.950
was decidedly not a charmer. “I must break
you.”

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:41.460
The film Red Heat really won the villain jackpot
in its depiction of Soviet drug lord, Viktor

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:46.579
Rostavili, who, in a trifecta of evil, also
murdered the protagonist’s partner. But

00:10:46.579 --> 00:10:51.420
arguably the clearest example of 1980s Cold
War villains came with 1984’s Red Dawn,

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:56.620
which imagined Soviet forces invading America
and worse, taking photos in our national parks.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:11:00.990
The film’s director, John Milius, explicitly
saw the film as a warning to Americans to

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:06.280
take seriously the threat of a Soviet invasion
via Central America. And if this seems a scare-monger

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:11.700
for you, consider that Milius was the direct
inspiration for this guy: "Smokey my friend,

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:14.630
you are entering a world of pain."

00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:19.519
Despite some return to “the other as the
enemy”, there are hints of the 70s skepticism

00:11:19.519 --> 00:11:24.770
towards institutions in the 1980s - when villains
are not pure caricatures from a Nancy Reagan

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:31.450
fever dream, we see them continue exploiting
corrupt institutions for personal gain. Gordon

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:35.450
“Greed is Good” Gecko, for instance, uses
the corrupt financial industry to fulfill

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:40.470
his own gold-plated desires. Meanwhile, Die
Hard’s Hans Gruber emerges as one of the

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:44.620
decade’s most enduring villains when he
and his cronies seize a high-rise building

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:49.279
and take hostages during a Christmas party.
He operates under the guise of being a terrorist

00:11:49.279 --> 00:11:51.601
exacting revenge on an evil corporation. "Due
to the Nakatomi Corporation’s legacy of

00:11:51.601 --> 00:12:00.270
greed around the globe... they're about to
be taught a lesson in the real use of power."

00:12:00.270 --> 00:12:03.060
Interestingly enough, his moral high ground
crumbles when it’s revealed that, just like

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:09.010
Gordon Gekko, he’s really only after cold
hard cash. "So that's is what this is about,

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:16.839
Hans? A fucking robbery? Why'd you have to
nuke the whole building, Hans?" "Well, when

00:12:16.839 --> 00:12:22.660
you steal $600, you can just disappear. When
you steal 600 million, they will find you,

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:24.850
unless they think you're already dead."

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:29.440
In the 90s, American cinema swapped out the
reds for homegrown terrorist to fit" a decade

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:35.000
in which domestic terrorism and “going postal”
dominated headlines. Fittingly, in cinema,

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:39.810
it was an era of disgruntled middle class
dudes and “bombers next door,” a prime-time

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:45.019
for complex anti-villains who are fighting
small, localized battles by radical means.

00:12:45.019 --> 00:12:47.810
Take the villain from Speed, Howard Payne
as portrayed by Dennis Hopper. This former

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:52.810
bomb squad-officer turned-extortionist turned
psychopathic terrorist is partially motivated

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:58.209
by a sweet 3.7 million dollars, but he also
expresses a sense of superiority. "You still

00:12:58.209 --> 00:13:07.570
don’t get it, do you Jack, huh? The beauty
of it. A bomb is made to explode. That’s

00:13:07.570 --> 00:13:09.430
its meaning its purpose." That’s... bananas.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:15.380
But it’s a hint of philosophy, of motivation
beyond cold hard cash. Howard’s a hint more

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:19.760
complicated than say Gruber in that he genuinely
(it seems) desires “meaning” and “purpose.”

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:24.320
He just goes about getting them in the worst
way possible. Same goes for Kevin Spacey’s

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:30.400
supremely sadistic John Doe in the 1995 film
Se7en, who sees himself as “chosen” warrior

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:38.959
in the crusade against everyday sin. “We
see a deadly sin on every street corner and

00:13:38.959 --> 00:13:49.980
we tolerate it because it's common. Well not
anymore. I’m setting the example.”

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:54.720
By the end of the decade, Arlington Road became
the pinnacle of the domestic terror genre,

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:58.839
whose villain mans the grill at the block
party as he carries out plans for his militant

00:13:58.839 --> 00:14:09.110
group’s latest deadly bombing. He claims
to be pursuing a lofty noble goal: "I'm a

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:10.110
messenger, Michael. I'm a messenger! There's
millions of us, waiting to take up arms…

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:12.550
ready to spread the word." But within his
critique of government is also an indictment

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:19.600
of the supposed niceties of Clinton-era prosperity.
"Are you happy in your godless, suburban life?"

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:24.430
Trouble in suburbia also abounded in American
Beauty, which depicted the enemy as Lester’s

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:30.170
neighbor, the homophobic, Nazi-memorabilia-hoarding
Frank Fitts, who kills Lester after he rebuffs

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:35.079
his romantic advancements. In both cases,
the villains feel victimized by the system

00:14:35.079 --> 00:14:40.521
or circumstance, and seek justice on their
own icky terms. Sometimes these villains even

00:14:40.521 --> 00:14:45.500
have genuinely noble causes, as they rail
against the systems that have wronged them.

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:50.790
Take 1995’s The Rock, in which Ed Harris’s
General Hummel, a disgraced USMC brigadier

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:55.490
general, who holds the entire city of San
Francisco hostage, — “Fifteen vx gas rockets

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:59.139
at the heart of San Fran. You’ve got 17
hours to deliver the money.” — demanding

00:14:59.139 --> 00:15:02.630
that the families of slain Marines be compensated
for their deaths.

00:15:02.630 --> 00:15:07.829
Here, we come to an interesting question of
whether noble ends justify violent means.

00:15:07.829 --> 00:15:12.389
In this way, the film acts as a prelude to
the very questions being explored by current-day

00:15:12.389 --> 00:15:16.779
villains, though usually on a much wider-scale.
Movies couldn’t not be changed by an event

00:15:16.779 --> 00:15:20.279
as pivotal as 9/11 and the subsequent War
on Terror.

00:15:20.279 --> 00:15:24.930
With the 2000s, came a new shift in movie
villains, as America confronted an existential

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:29.380
threat to its sense of safety and power in
the world. Fittingly, the first two installments

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:33.699
of Christopher Nolan’s Dark Knight Trilogy
captured the popular imagination in this decade.

00:15:33.699 --> 00:15:38.730
In 2005’s “Batman Begins,” we see the
dangerous metropolis under attack by international

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:44.529
terrorist group “The League of Shadows,”
and its leader, Raz Al Ghul who in a remarkably-on-the-nose

00:15:44.529 --> 00:15:48.220
metaphor, wants to blow up Gotham’s tallest
skyscraper.

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:52.820
Arguably the decade's most salient villain
came, of course, with Heath Ledger’s portrayal

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:57.420
of The Joker in The Dark Knight. The Joker
manifests as “the spirit of terrorism,"

00:15:57.420 --> 00:16:02.070
the embodiment “of anarchy and chaos of
a particularly destructive and nihilistic

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:06.190
nature,” as critic Douglas Kellner writes
in his book Cinema Wars. “The only sensible

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:08.120
way to live in this world is without rules.”

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:12.199
In case the comparison between the Joker and
real world terrorism wasn’t clear enough,

00:16:12.199 --> 00:16:16.949
Kellner notes that the film is bursting with
9/11 iconography. Nolan’s film also gave

00:16:16.949 --> 00:16:21.829
us one of two coin-flipping evil-doers with
the character of Harvey Dent alias Two-Face.

00:16:21.829 --> 00:16:27.240
A representation of how confrontation with
the Joker’s evils can corrupt even the noblest

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:32.079
person, Two-Face goes about “deciding people’s
fates through a plunge into a completely meaningless

00:16:32.079 --> 00:16:35.269
existence of pure contingency and nihilism.

00:16:35.269 --> 00:16:40.209
Meanwhile, the Coen Brothers’ Anton Chigurh
from No Country For Old Men also uses a coin

00:16:40.209 --> 00:16:44.850
to decide the fate of his victims. He doesn’t
kill out of greed, self-defense or some kind

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:49.410
of practical goal, leading one character to
comment. “He is a peculiar man. Might even

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:55.810
say he has principles, principles that transcend
money or drugs.”

00:16:55.810 --> 00:17:00.630
Indeed, Chigurh is quite simply an active
nihilist who believes that, if all morality

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:05.920
is contrived, a coin flip is as good a way
as any to decide who lives or dies. But you

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:10.740
don’t need a coin to affirm the random or
meaningless nature of our lives or deaths.

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:15.170
Take Martin Scorsese's 2006 film, The Departed,
which featured the inimitable Jack Nicholson

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:21.569
as chief gangster Frank Costello. From merciless
killing —&nbsp;"She fell funny." — to his

00:17:21.569 --> 00:17:27.709
refusal to subscribe to any rules. "I don’t
want my society to shape me, I want to shape

00:17:27.709 --> 00:17:35.720
my society." Frank actions seem shaped by
the belief that life is meaningless. "How’s

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:41.590
your mother?" "She’s on her way out." "We
all are, act accordingly.” Frank, who we

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:46.720
later find out was an FBI informant, sees
life as one long struggle during which any

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:51.050
actions that sustain you for just one more
day are entirely excusable.

00:17:51.050 --> 00:17:56.640
Of course, plenty of other villain archetypes
punctuated the 2000s. But if the 2000s were

00:17:56.640 --> 00:18:01.020
a time when the villainous creed was no creed
at all, we’ve come a long way since then.

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:05.460
Today, our villains are the product of an
increasingly divided America bracing itself

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:11.070
for impact. With trust in government, media
and religious faith at all all-time low, villains

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:16.631
reflect America’s innate skepticism towards
systems, any systems, all the systems! Villains

00:18:16.631 --> 00:18:22.220
simply embody solutions that are way too radical
and usually mind-bogglingly violent.

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:27.460
The polar opposite of a 1950s villain, who
represented an evildoer infiltrating an inherently

00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:32.290
good system in hopes of corrupting it, today’s
villains increasingly face objectively evil

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:36.400
systems which they want to change for the
better. The only thing that makes these characters

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:48.070
villains rather than starry-eyed heroes is
their means of carrying out that change,

00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:51.590
the classic caveat of “you’d be really
cool if you weren’t trying to kill half

00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:52.590
of the world."

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:56.340
For instance, Black Panther’s Killmonger,
played by Michael B. Jordan, has an entirely

00:18:56.340 --> 00:19:00.780
legitimate critique of how the world treats
its black citizens and Wakanda’s passivity

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:05.000
as they suffer. In fact, Killmonger would
argue that the real villains of the film are

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:09.720
Wakandans who sit on a stockpile of vibranium
while pretending to be a helpless Third World

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:14.360
country. Where Killmonger’s perspective
gets debatably tricky is in the means - he

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:19.380
wants to ship Wakandan weapons all over the
world and instigate a militant uprising. "The

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:25.860
world's gonna start over, and this time, we're
on top. The sun will never set on the Wakandan

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:26.860
empire."

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:31.360
Though T’Challa defeats him and stops the
scheme, he does go to the UN to admit Wakanda’s

00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:36.240
real capacities, and even establishes a Wakandian
outreach center in Oakland, suggesting an

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:42.100
end to Wakanda’s isolationism. So Killmonger’s
radicalism actually brought about a less radical,

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:43.730
but positive change in policy.

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:49.240
Then, there’s Mission Impossible 6’s new
utilitarian villain, August Walker/John Lark,

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:53.690
who wants to destroy ⅓ of the world in the
hopes that the other ⅔ would come together

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:58.350
in the wake of the attacks. Or as he says:
“There cannot be peace without, first, a

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:03.020
great suffering and the greater the suffering,
the greater the peace.” Or Infinity War’s

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:06.840
Thanos, who wants to kill half the world to
solve resource scarcities that plague the

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:11.340
universe. Or The Incredibles 2’s villain
Screenslaver, who uses hypnotic screens to

00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:16.020
mind-control her minions. Her personal beef
with the system? That the endless repackaging

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:21.860
of life into consumable media has made us
all lazy passive observers, and that superheroes

00:20:21.860 --> 00:20:26.490
are an integral part of the equation keeping
us docile and meek. Her plan to change the

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:29.210
system: mind control superheroes, cause a
huge accident and ruin superhero’s public

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:33.270
approval ratings so people will learn that
they have to protect themselves.

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:38.360
What all these villains share: A majorly out-there
plan that appeals to our country’s growing

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:44.650
fears that the world is screwed beyond repair,
and the only way to fix it is to tear it down.

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:48.420
In this brave new world of villainy, we’re
able to empathize with the villain’s critique

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:52.370
of the system, and their desire to change
it. In this “through the looking glass”

00:20:52.370 --> 00:20:56.970
reinterpretation of villainy, the heroes are
almost inevitably cast as slightly-flawed

00:20:56.970 --> 00:21:00.370
protectors of a status quo that nobody’s
really happy with.

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:05.150
In the grand scheme of things, contemporary
villains seem to be a weird mirror image of

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:09.170
50’s villains, who want to corrupt good
institutions – Killmonger-types want to

00:21:09.170 --> 00:21:14.549
affect good change in corrupt institutions
- they just go about it in very bad ways.

00:21:14.549 --> 00:21:19.120
At the same time, our contemporary villains
seem to have merged the complexity and sometimes,

00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:24.140
apparent innocence of 90s villains while broadening
the social critiques of the 70s, and standing

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:28.809
in opposition to the nihilism of the 2000s.
They do believe that there is a cause worth

00:21:28.809 --> 00:21:33.450
fighting for, and if they drafted different
blueprints for creating social change, they

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:37.460
might just resurface as… complex heroes.
So what do you think?

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:41.200
Are today’s villains more interesting than
past evildoers? And what do you think will

00:21:41.200 --> 00:22:04.049
come next for Hollywood bad guys? Let us know
in the comments and

00:22:04.049 --> 00:22:13.529
as always, peace!

